Draw the **dogsled**, a 64x32 (two-tile) hazard sprite for an arctic crossing game,
using only the drawing tool.

The dogsled is a hazard: a team of husky sled dogs pulling a musher's sled at speed
across the lanes the player must cross. It must read at a glance as a fast-moving
sled team — a vehicle racing across the ice to dodge — the quick, light counterpart
to the big slow snow plow, not a lone creature or a safe platform. Draw it running
right (dogs leading, sled trailing), filling the 64x32 canvas, on full
transparency.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw` binary already on your `PATH`. It is the
sole channel: you build the sprite by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image produced
any other way (writing a PNG directly, editing `canvas.png` by hand) is discarded
and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

After each call the binary re-renders the current image to `canvas.png` and appends
the operation to `actions.json`. A blank starting canvas and an empty log are
already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
draw fill-rect --x 38 --y 14 --width 12 --height 8 --color "#d8dde0"
```

Run `draw --help` to list every operation and `draw <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`canvas.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next.

